lowing the client's chosen retirement age of 60 to be implemented. Income drawdown permits regular payments, lump sums, or phased drawdown [P15]. The client plans to use pension to clear £33,000 in unsecured liabilities (credit card £7,000 and personal loan £13,000) [P17, P21].","discrepancies":[" Report asserts retirement income objective of '£25,000 to £30,000 in retirement' but Fact Find budget notes describe personal expenditure of £2,000 net per month (£24,000 annually), not £25,000-£30,000. Unclear whether the stated retirement income is personal gross, household income including partner contribution, or net.","Report states personal expenditure in retirement as £2,000 net per month, but separately records overall household expenses as £2,800 before accounting for partner's £800 contribution, creating ambiguity about which figure represents the basis for affordability.","Report plans to withdraw £40,000 tax-free lump sum comprising £20,000 for emergency funds and legal fees and £20,000 to pay off liabilities, but the total liabilities recorded are £88,000 (mortgage £68,000, credit card £7,000, personal loan £13,000), with only the unsecured portion (£20,000) being addressed by the recommendation. Mortgage remains outstanding for 14 years until age 65 at £600 monthly.","Client's emergency fund shortfall is £17,000 (current £7,000 vs required £24,000). The recommendation proposes £20,000 from pension as emergency fund replacement, but this does not clearly restore the fund to the required £24,000 if any portion is depleted before pension drawdown stabilises.","Report discusses inheritance of £350,000 pending legal proceedings, expected within one year, which materially affects financial resilience post-recommendation, but this expectation is not integrated into the baseline affordability assessment or emergency fund adequacy assessment."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Clarification of whether the stated retirement income objective of '£25,000 to £