d. Second, the emergency fund issue: the client requires £24,000 but currently holds £7,000, a shortfall of £17,000 [P21]. The recommendation allocates £20,000 from the tax-free lump sum to 'replace emergency funds', but the Fact Find notes indicate the fund will likely be down to £4,000 by implementation date [P21], meaning the £20,000 would barely restore the fund to target. No assessment is provided of whether £24,000 is adequate once income drawdown begins, given the 12-month coverage ratio the client expressed and the fact that income from pension (£1,900) plus partner contribution (£800) equals £2,700 against baseline expenditure, leaving only £437 monthly surplus before considering liabilities (mortgage £600, card £175, loan £308 = £1,083, which exceeds the stated personal expenditure). Third, the liabilities position: the client carries £88,000 in total liabilities (mortgage £68,000, card £7,000, loan £13,000) [P17]. The recommendation clears only the unsecured debt (£20,000 from the lump sum), leaving the mortgage at £600 monthly for 14 years until age 65, meaning the client will still have a £68,000 liability when she reaches age 60. This is material to cashflow: if personal expenditure is £2,000 and mortgage continues at £600, the true baseline is £2,600, not £2,000, and the surplus from pension income (£1,900) and partner (£800) becomes £100 monthly before any discretionary spending. The inheritance expectation (£350,000, pending legal proceedings, expected within one year) is discussed in objectives but not integrated into the affordability baseline, creating ambiguity about whether the current emergency fund target and liquidity assessment remain valid if the inheritance arrives as expected. The report does not clearly separate pre-inheritance and post-inheritance scenarios. No evidence is presented that the client's support needs, communication requirements or vulnerability characteristics (if any) have been considered in relation to the complexity of managing ongoing drawdown income and the risk of insufficient cashflow if income expectations (partner's £800 contribution, own employment income of £20,000 from 2027) fail to materialise.","citations":[{"passageId":"P21","source":"Fact Find","category":"B","quote":"Melanie overall expenses are in the region of £2,800, this is accounting for all expenses such as holidays etc. She has stopped working and therefore lost employment income, she has stated that she would lie to replace with income from the pension of £1,900 per month. With the additional partner contribution of £800 towards bills this will put her very close to overall expenditure for the month with a slight reduction in luxuries, allowing her to live comfortably.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Fact Find","category":"B","quote":"She is also wanting to withdraw a tax free cash lump sum of £40,000. £20,000 to replace emergency funds and also to cover some legal fees for the ongoing issue she has regarding the inheritance. The other £20,000 is to be used to pay off the personal loan and CC liabilities.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Fact Find","category":"B","quote":"Currently living off savings which is now reduced to £7,000 and this also acts as an emergency pot. She prefers to have 12 months of expenditure as an emergency reserve so close to £24,000. The £7,000 will likely be down to circa £4,000 by the time the pension income is set up and this is making her feel uncomfortable.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Fact Find","category":"B","quote":"Amount put aside for Emergency Fund £ 7,000.00 Amount of Emergency Fund Required £ 24,000.00 Emergency Fund Shortfall £ 17,000.00","cells":["£7,000.00","£24,000.00","£17,000.00"]},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Fact Find","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Home Mortgage","£68,000.00","Credit Card on 0%","£7,000.00","Personal Loan","£13,000.00"]},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Fact Find","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["£6