{"groupId":"CHK-007","requirement":"[G7.1] All material costs disclosed — product, platform, fund, transaction, advice. Limb: Consistency. Where costs, charges and the value of recommendations are referenced within the Suitability Report, does the Suitability Report disclose all material charges relevant to the recommendation, including applicable initial, ongoing, product, platform, investment and transaction costs?","reportSays":"The suitability report discloses the following cost layers for the recommended Aviva Platform Personal Pension: Platform 0.19% ongoing, Investment Management 0.20% ongoing, for a Total Charge of 0.39% pa. In monetary terms, this equates to £623.10 platform charges and £655.89 investment management charges, totalling £1,278.99 annually on the fund value. Initial adviser fee is 3.00% (£9,838.36). Ongoing adviser fee is 1.00% pa (£265.09 per month estimated). The report states that transaction costs of underlying funds are excluded because they are variable and subject to change based on market conditions and trading activity, and notes these are already accounted for in fund performance figures presented net of all charges. Product and platform charges are disclosed.","fileSays":"Fund factsheets (Vanguard LifeStrategy 80% Equity and HSBC Global Strategy Dynamic Portfolio C Acc) show OCF/TER of 0.20% and 0.19% respectively. Aviva pension charges documentation shows HSBC Global Strategy Dynamic Portfolio C Acc TER/OCF of 0.19% and Vanguard LifeStrategy 80% Equity Ongoing charge of 0.20%, with no initial charges. Aviva illustration shows initial adviser charge of 3.00% equalling £9,838.36 deducted from cash transfer payments totalling £327,945.44, and ongoing adviser charge of 1.00% payable monthly. The Aviva pension illustration shows Platform charge (Aviva) but does not separately itemise a platform charge figure; it shows Investment Management charges for the funds themselves. Fund charges data shows weighted average fund charges across True Potential existing arrangements but no platform charge is specified for True Potential (stated as 0.40% in existing arrangements, but this includes adviser charge 0.50% not yet incurred, creating ambiguity in the basis).","discrepancies":["Report states Platform charge 0.19% and Investment Management 0.20% totalling 0.39% for recommended plan. Aviva documentation shows fund OCF/TER of 0.19% and 0.20% respectively, but does not separately state a 0.19% platform charge as a distinct layer—the charges appear to be fund charges, not platform plus fund charges. The decomposition of 0.39% into Platform 0.19% and Investment Management 0.20% cannot be reconciled from the Aviva charges documentation, which treats these as fund OCF figures, not platform + fund split.","Report's initial adviser fee of 3.00% (£9,838.36) is calculated on transfer values of £327,945.44. Verification: 3% × £327,945.44 = £9,838.36. This is arithmetically correct. However, the canonical model notes identify that £9,838.36 ÷ £67,945.44 (transfer value of two arrangements only) = 14.49%, creating ambiguity about which base was used. The report calculates 3% on the full £327,945.44 current value, not the £67,945.44 being transferred. The basis discrepancy is unresolved in the evidence.","Aviva illustration confirms initial adviser charge 3.00% = £9,838.36 on £327,945.44 total cash transfer, and ongoing adviser charge 1.00% payable monthly (£265.09 estimated). Report states ongoing adviser fee is 1.00% pa. These align.","Transaction costs are stated as excluded 'because they are variable and subject to change based on factors such as market conditions and trading activity.' The report discloses this exclusion with reasons. The fund factsheets (Vanguard, HSBC) and Aviva KFD state that ongoing charges figures 'exclude portfolio transaction costs.' This is consistent disclosure of a material exclusion with reasons provided."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The requirement tests whether all material cost layers are disclosed and consistent. The report discloses six material cost components: (1) Platform charge 0.19% pa, (2) Investment Management charge 0.20% pa, (3) Initial adviser fee 3.00%, (4) Ongoing adviser fee 1.00% pa, and (5) an explicit exclusion of transaction costs with stated reasons. All are present in the report's cost disclosure section. The Aviva pension charges document and fund factsheets corroborate the fund OCF figures of 0.19% (HSBC) and 0.20% (Vanguard). The Aviva illustration corroborates the adviser fees (3.00% initial = £9,838.36, and 1.00% ongoing). However, a material ambiguity arises in the composition of the 0.39% total charge. The report presents this as Platform 0.19% + Investment Management 0.20% = 0.39% pa. The Aviva charges document shows the HSBC fund at 0.19% OCF and Vanguard fund at 0.20% OCF, but does not separately identify a 0.19% 'platform charge' as a distinct layer. The Aviva illustration references 'Aviva charge' but directs readers to separate Key Features Documents for details, stating only 'For details of your Aviva charges, please refer to your Key features illustration(s), provided separately.' The KFD itself states ongoing charges of 0.19% and 0.20% but labels these as fund charges (OCF), not platform fees. The report's decomposition treats the 0.39% as a two-layer stack (Platform + Investment Management), but the evidence suggests these may be fund OCF figures only, with any platform charge either embedded, separately accounted, or not itemised in the passages provided. This creates a basis ambiguity: is the report stating Platform fee 0.19% separately, or conflating fund management charges with platform infrastructure? The Aviva T&Cs reference platform fees in context of discretionary model charges and DIM arrangements, but do not quantify a baseline platform fee of 0.19% in the passages retrieved. The initial advice fee of 3.00% = £9,838.36 is arithmetically sound when applied to the £327,945.44 total current value across all three existing arrangements. However, the canonical model ambiguity flags that this may have been calculated on a different base (£67,945.44 transfer value only = 14.49%), suggesting potential confusion in the original report about which assets the fee applies to. The illustration clarifies the fee is deducted from 'cash transfer payments' totalling £327,945.44, suggesting it is applied to total assets held, not transfer value alone. Transaction costs are properly disclosed as excluded, with reasons, consistent with fund factsheet language. The cumulative effect of charges is stated in monetary terms (£1,278.99 pa) and the illustration provides projected values showing the impact of charges over time. Aggregation is present. The comparison can be performed because the fund charges are corroborated. The ambiguity about platform charge decomposition does not prevent comparison—it reveals that the basis of the report's cost split is not fully supported by the evidence provided. This is a disclosure clarity issue: the report claims a 0.19% platform charge but the evidence does not isolate such a charge as a separate platform fee; it shows fund OCF figures only.","citations":[{"passageId":"P10","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":"Annual recurring charges: Platform 0.19%, Investment Management 0.20%, Total Charge 0.39%","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":"The charges quoted in this report exclude the transaction costs of the underlying funds, as these are variable and subject to change based on factors such as market conditions and trading activity.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Anness, M Aviva pension charges 24.3.26.md","category":"H_ProductDocumentation","quote":null,"cells":["HSBC Global Strategy Dynamic Portfolio C Acc","0.19%","TER/OCF"]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Anness, M Aviva